**Abstract** Cardiac fibrosis contributes to progressive heart failure through the accumulation of activated fibroblasts and excessive extracellular matrix remodeling. In this study, we engineer human pluripotent stem cell (hPSC)-derived neutrophils expressing a chimeric antigen receptor (CAR) targeting fibroblast activation protein (FAP). FAP CAR-neutrophils demonstrate enhanced reactive oxygen species-associated responses and dose-dependent cytotoxicity against activated fibroblasts in vitro. In three-dimensional cardiac organoids, they reduce fibroblast-associated signals without significantly decreasing cardiomyocyte-associated cardiac troponin T staining. The engineered neutrophils also exhibit an N1-associated phenotype characterized by increased iNOS and reduced Arginase-1 expression. Single-cell transcriptomic analysis of cardiac injury datasets informs therapeutic timing, while exploratory experiments in a murine myocardial infarction model show improved left ventricular ejection fraction following systemic FAP CAR-neutrophil administration. These findings establish a proof of concept for hPSC-derived CAR-neutrophils as a potential cell-based immunotherapy targeting cardiac fibrosis.

**Keywords:** CAR-neutrophils, human pluripotent stem cells, cardiac fibrosis, fibroblast activation protein, FAP, immunotherapy, myocardial infarction, cardiac organoids, cell engineering

